[SPEAKER_00]: In the same sense, you know, having a fever, when you catch a virus, it's automatic.

343
00:31:15,510 --> 00:31:18,032
[SPEAKER_00]: But, what if you learn how to prevent catching a virus?

344
00:31:18,292 --> 00:31:19,293
[SPEAKER_00]: You won't get a fever.

345
00:31:19,813 --> 00:31:31,441
[SPEAKER_00]: So, having a mind move and respond, even if you don't want to and you can't catch it in time, means you are doing something or maintaining something, on some level, that then makes that mind overwhelmed.

346
00:31:32,042 --> 00:31:36,225
[SPEAKER_00]: And the very first thing, it's in the last talk we posted now, it comes down to that.

347
00:31:37,243 --> 00:31:40,424
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether your value is towards management or towards uprooting.

348
00:31:41,944 --> 00:31:49,126
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you think you shouldn't be saying this, you shouldn't be doing that, that's on the level of management, which, as I said, everybody starts like that.

349
00:31:49,746 --> 00:31:51,066
[SPEAKER_00]: But really, you want to see...

350
00:31:51,086 --> 00:31:52,166
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't want to be affected.

351
00:31:52,206 --> 00:31:58,168
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether I choose to speak or not, I should not be affected emotionally by what was said to me.

352
00:31:59,240 --> 00:32:00,821
[SPEAKER_00]: And for that, you are responsible.

353
00:32:00,961 --> 00:32:06,483
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you realize, that is the basis for my mind automatically blurting things out that I shouldn't have said.

354
00:32:07,183 --> 00:32:08,163
[SPEAKER_00]: I was affected.

355
00:32:08,943 --> 00:32:10,924
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how can then I not be affected?

356
00:32:11,044 --> 00:32:13,005
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, I don't have to worry what mind will say.

357
00:32:14,533 --> 00:32:19,199
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it won't be rooted in self-defense, gratuitously, or something like that.

358
00:32:19,880 --> 00:32:21,262
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means it won't be unwholesome.

359
00:32:21,742 --> 00:32:30,354
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why Arahant doesn't need to worry about his actions, in a sense, because he knows whatever he chooses to do, will not be rooted in greed, aversion, or distraction, or delusion.

360
00:32:31,324 --> 00:32:33,085
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in that sense, it's not automatic.

361
00:32:33,125 --> 00:32:34,365
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean.

362
00:32:34,425 --> 00:32:36,185
[SPEAKER_00]: Mind blurting stuff, it's automatic.

363
00:32:36,305 --> 00:32:44,788
[SPEAKER_00]: But you, through your other choices, when you're not affected, are maintaining the basis which then makes the mind blurt things out verbally.

364
00:32:45,648 --> 00:32:47,249
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, for some people, physically as well.

365
00:32:51,530 --> 00:32:52,770
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay.

366
00:32:57,432 --> 00:32:58,092
[SPEAKER_02]: So...

367
00:32:59,650 --> 00:33:03,632
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, I'm kind of jumping many subjects.

368
00:33:06,393 --> 00:33:12,355
[SPEAKER_02]: But, yeah, no, I actually listened to your last talk yesterday.

369
00:33:13,856 --> 00:33:27,821
[SPEAKER_02]: And, yeah, I agree with the point that it's not enough to just become angry, let's say, when somebody says something that

370
00:33:28,560 --> 00:33:29,841
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's another thing, by the way.

371
00:33:29,861 --> 00:33:33,643
[SPEAKER_02]: If the mind automatically blurs stuff out, means you haven't developed restraint sufficiently.

372
00:33:53,151 --> 00:34:02,317
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter how pressing, whatever you want to defend yourself against, the choice of not acting out by body, by speech, by mind, always remains there.

373
00:34:02,357 --> 00:34:07,181
[SPEAKER_00]: The fact that you acted out, the mind said something or thought something, means you need to develop more.

374
00:34:08,308 --> 00:34:17,112
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to be able to hold it more without actually letting... and the more you become aware of that lack, so to speak, the more you'll be able to develop it.

375
00:34:17,152 --> 00:34:29,279
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you think, not you specifically, but if a person might think, oh, the speech happened automatically, means you will not look for an upgrade there, because you don't feel responsible for it now.

376
00:34:29,379 --> 00:34:29,559
[SPEAKER_00]: See?

377
00:34:29,679 --> 00:34:30,919
[SPEAKER_00]: You said, oh, it's automatic.

378
00:34:31,280 --> 00:34:31,940
[SPEAKER_00]: Wasn't my fault.

379
00:34:32,240 --> 00:34:35,322
[SPEAKER_00]: My fault is later, or avoiding my fault is later what I do.

380
00:34:35,682 --> 00:34:35,842
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

381
00:34:36,948 --> 00:34:41,754
[SPEAKER_00]: It was automatic, but there is a certain basis for mind acting automatically.

382
00:34:42,395 --> 00:34:43,816
[SPEAKER_00]: Basis that lacks the restraint.

383
00:34:44,297 --> 00:34:48,963
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can even... doesn't matter how automatic it is, you can always choose no.

384
00:34:49,904 --> 00:34:52,327
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the restraint, and then the wisdom can come out of it.

385
00:34:53,462 --> 00:34:57,906
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even in a common sense of sense restraint, there is still something to be done there.

386
00:34:58,566 --> 00:35:01,969
[SPEAKER_00]: Not necessarily, I must discern this basis and why am I affected in the first place.

387
00:35:02,009 --> 00:35:04,771
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's all on the level of vipassana and developing wisdom.

388
00:35:05,412 --> 00:35:09,355
[SPEAKER_00]: But even if you don't get any of them, you can still keep your mouth shut, literally.

389
00:35:10,556 --> 00:35:14,859
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you blurt out, means your verbal restraint hasn't been strengthened enough.

390
00:35:18,482 --> 00:35:19,523
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, eh...

391
00:35:23,282 --> 00:35:25,624
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, I mean, like, it's still, I agree, it's still your fault.

392
00:35:25,664 --> 00:35:35,210
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, if you still, if you kind of blurt out words automatically, then it's still your fault, but it's your fault kind of in the sense that you were not...

393
00:35:36,568 --> 00:35:40,170
[SPEAKER_02]: You were not mindful enough and you were not restrained enough at that point.

394
00:35:40,210 --> 00:35:43,091
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, and from the point of view of one who practices, that's a big fault.

395
00:35:43,711 --> 00:35:45,792
[SPEAKER_00]: Without fixing that fault, there is no progress.

396
00:35:46,372 --> 00:35:47,713
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not like...

397
00:35:47,733 --> 00:35:53,315
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, I mean, sure, it's not as bad as somebody who has no interest in practicing dhamma, but you don't want to be comparing to that side of things.

398
00:35:54,215 --> 00:35:56,497
[SPEAKER_00]: So, restraint has not been developed.

399
00:35:56,537 --> 00:36:02,382
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, wisdom cannot arise in regard to speech, in regard to being affected by what other people say.

400
00:36:03,162 --> 00:36:09,047
[SPEAKER_00]: I cannot uproot my emotional kind of vulnerability, so to speak, until I fix this first.

401
00:36:09,908 --> 00:36:10,868
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

402
00:36:10,969 --> 00:36:13,731
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not automatic at all, in a sense.

403
00:36:13,751 --> 00:36:22,578
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, it's just a symptom for which you're not directly responsible, in the sense of you are not generating the symptom, but you're fully responsible for the reason of that symptom.

404
00:36:24,857 --> 00:36:25,577
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah.

405
00:36:25,717 --> 00:36:42,745
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, yeah, when I say automatic, I kind of... it's a way of saying that, like, even though, like, you don't... yeah, it's the way of saying that you kind of don't deliberately sort of decide, now I will speak these words, because you know that you're not supposed to say them.

406
00:36:43,666 --> 00:36:44,486
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean.

407
00:36:44,506 --> 00:36:54,311
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to actually start kind of fabricating and investing effort to develop responsibility for things that don't necessarily feel they are your fault.

408
00:36:55,533 --> 00:36:58,135
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's the only way to then actually approve things correctly.

409
00:37:03,639 --> 00:37:19,752
[SPEAKER_02]: And, especially in regard to speech, it's a... like, it's an interesting point, because... like, this... because we have said and we both agree that you...

410
00:37:20,425 --> 00:37:28,791
[SPEAKER_02]: If you are... if you are speaking things without considering, and then you then have regret later, then it's... it means that you're doing something wrong.

411
00:37:28,811 --> 00:37:34,815
[SPEAKER_02]: It means that you haven't developed enough... enough restraint in regard to your speech, and you don't have enough mindfulness.

412
00:37:35,495 --> 00:37:48,744
[SPEAKER_02]: But, like, the kind of mindfulness that needs... this is the point I'm trying to come to, actually, is the... the type of mindfulness that needs to be there in order to be able to practice restraint in regards to the words that you're saying.

413
00:37:49,174 --> 00:38:00,862
[SPEAKER_02]: Obviously, it can't be just about being aware of, like, the sensation of sitting on the chair, or being, like, mindful of the... like, something outside object of your senses.

414
00:38:02,583 --> 00:38:15,032
[SPEAKER_02]: But, like, so from... from my understanding, like, you need to be aware... basically, you need to be aware of your own mind, like, the... of how, like, the... of the fact that

415
00:38:16,488 --> 00:38:20,451
[SPEAKER_02]: You're hearing these words, and now, what happens in your mind as you hear them?

416
00:38:21,852 --> 00:38:25,976
[SPEAKER_02]: Does the mind react with anger or not?

417
00:38:27,898 --> 00:38:33,964
[SPEAKER_02]: And now, even if it's reacting in anger, then if you are aware at that point, then you're able to stop it.

418
00:38:34,584 --> 00:38:36,646
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you are not aware, then you're not able to stop it.

419
00:38:36,666 --> 00:38:38,027
[SPEAKER_02]: Or, if you're more aware, then you can

420
00:38:41,239 --> 00:38:51,571
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how would you then go about being aware of your mind?

421
00:38:51,731 --> 00:38:52,672
[SPEAKER_00]: How would you do that then?

422
00:38:53,854 --> 00:38:58,980
[SPEAKER_00]: How would you then not lose awareness of your mind, so then you don't say something out of carelessness?

423
00:39:01,815 --> 00:39:06,680
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, if somebody is... but you need to just maintain awareness of your mind, essentially.

424
00:39:06,700 --> 00:39:07,981
[SPEAKER_02]: It's something that you need to practice.

425
00:39:08,381 --> 00:39:10,764
[SPEAKER_00]: No, but say I don't know what awareness of the mind is.

426
00:39:11,384 --> 00:39:14,387
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, in other words, it might mean one thing for me, another thing for you.

427
00:39:15,268 --> 00:39:19,272
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how can I understand what awareness of the mind is for you?

428
00:39:19,332 --> 00:39:20,653
[SPEAKER_00]: How can I practically do it?

429
00:39:21,554 --> 00:39:23,656
[SPEAKER_00]: I want to be aware of the mind the way you're describing.

430
00:39:23,756 --> 00:39:25,138
[SPEAKER_00]: How can I go about and do that?

431
00:39:27,920 --> 00:39:38,406
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, it's like not to look at what you see and what you hear, not that somebody is saying these words, but at that point, you have to maintain awareness of who hears.

432
00:39:38,586 --> 00:39:40,047
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, what is hearing?

433
00:39:40,067 --> 00:39:48,192
[SPEAKER_02]: At that place or at that point, who is hearing?

434
00:39:48,252 --> 00:39:49,012
[SPEAKER_02]: Who is speaking?

435
00:39:49,072 --> 00:39:49,633
[SPEAKER_02]: Who is acting?

436
00:39:49,733 --> 00:39:51,574
[SPEAKER_02]: Or who is listening?

437
00:39:53,857 --> 00:40:03,163
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, to be a... like, if you were aware at that point, now you know... like, to know... to know at the point where the words are... where the sound, example, is meeting the mind.

438
00:40:03,583 --> 00:40:05,505
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, because when somebody is saying words...

439
00:40:05,665 --> 00:40:06,465
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it is about...

440
00:40:06,646 --> 00:40:08,126
[SPEAKER_00]: So, hold on, hold on, hold on.

441
00:40:08,607 --> 00:40:14,130
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if it's about the point where the sound meets the mind, it is about things you see and hear.

442
00:40:14,331 --> 00:40:16,872
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you started by saying it's not about things you see and hear.

443
00:40:17,953 --> 00:40:20,695
[SPEAKER_00]: But if that's what you're paying attention, then it is about what you're hearing.

444
00:40:22,925 --> 00:40:32,089
[SPEAKER_02]: No, but what I mean is not to the... not paying attention to the sound itself, but paying attention to how it is formed in the mind, like, how it is heard.

445
00:40:32,609 --> 00:40:35,690
[SPEAKER_02]: The... like, for example, because I have...

446
00:40:35,710 --> 00:40:38,471
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, I cannot give many examples from my... from myself.

447
00:40:38,491 --> 00:40:43,453
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, there is... Well, it's heard as an insult.

448
00:40:43,493 --> 00:40:45,394
[SPEAKER_00]: For example, I heard an insult.

449
00:40:47,989 --> 00:40:53,471
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I was aware of hearing the sound entering my ears and everything else, but it was an insult.

450
00:40:56,092 --> 00:41:01,614
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how do I be aware of that, so I'm not insulted to begin with?

451
00:41:02,194 --> 00:41:09,197
[SPEAKER_02]: But the point is that it's an insult only if you're... like, it's your mind that takes it as an insult.

452
00:41:09,277 --> 00:41:10,297
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, I am insulted.

453
00:41:10,635 --> 00:41:12,196
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the mind took it already.

454
00:41:12,556 --> 00:41:14,938
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how do I... how do I not be affected by that?

455
00:41:15,078 --> 00:41:19,981
[SPEAKER_00]: Because everybody starts by already being insulted, affected, greedy, averse and so on.

456
00:41:22,123 --> 00:41:25,165
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how do I then not be affected by the insulted mind?

