[SPEAKER_00]: Is it the place that I'm affected?

518
00:45:54,400 --> 00:45:55,981
[SPEAKER_00]: It means, well, I am already affected.

519
00:45:56,001 --> 00:45:56,761
[SPEAKER_00]: I already know that.

520
00:45:56,821 --> 00:45:58,002
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't need to look for the place.

521
00:45:59,147 --> 00:46:00,267
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is the problem?

522
00:46:00,648 --> 00:46:01,468
[SPEAKER_00]: Let me put it like that.

523
00:46:01,588 --> 00:46:03,169
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the problem in being affected?

524
00:46:03,629 --> 00:46:05,710
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the problem in an insult?

525
00:46:06,410 --> 00:46:07,090
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it the words?

526
00:46:07,210 --> 00:46:07,891
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it the person?

527
00:46:07,931 --> 00:46:08,731
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it the attitude?

528
00:46:09,771 --> 00:46:11,232
[SPEAKER_00]: Even is it my conceit?

529
00:46:11,452 --> 00:46:11,592
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

530
00:46:11,712 --> 00:46:14,913
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the problem there and then, while I'm insulted?

531
00:46:19,535 --> 00:46:21,396
[SPEAKER_02]: What is the problem by being insulted?

532
00:46:21,536 --> 00:46:23,477
[SPEAKER_02]: The fact is that you feel insulted already.

533
00:46:24,766 --> 00:46:28,587
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but what is the problem of an insult?

534
00:46:28,647 --> 00:46:32,468
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, sometimes a person can say something to you and you're not insulted.

535
00:46:32,608 --> 00:46:35,189
[SPEAKER_00]: Sometimes they say the same thing and now you're insulted.

536
00:46:35,289 --> 00:46:36,829
[SPEAKER_00]: What's the difference between the two?

537
00:46:40,530 --> 00:46:46,671
[SPEAKER_02]: The difference is because, example, like, you can hear the same insulting words from in a movie, example.

538
00:46:48,772 --> 00:46:49,232
[SPEAKER_02]: Or, like,

539
00:46:50,232 --> 00:46:57,556
[SPEAKER_02]: In another context, and now you will not get insulted, because it's not... because I'm not... Yeah, the same context.

540
00:46:57,636 --> 00:47:01,177
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not relating two different contexts, which would be two different experiences.

541
00:47:01,237 --> 00:47:05,719
[SPEAKER_00]: The same experience, same context, same person, same occasion, same habit.

542
00:47:06,940 --> 00:47:10,902
[SPEAKER_00]: Once I feel insulted, another occasion I don't, which is quite possible.

543
00:47:11,282 --> 00:47:12,983
[SPEAKER_00]: What would be the difference between the two?

544
00:47:13,003 --> 00:47:14,183
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not the words I hear.

545
00:47:15,244 --> 00:47:16,345
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not even the mindset.

546
00:47:16,365 --> 00:47:19,346
[SPEAKER_00]: I do recognize, look, he wants to insult me like yesterday.

547
00:47:21,078 --> 00:47:23,300
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what would be the difference then, between the two?

548
00:47:26,824 --> 00:47:29,186
[SPEAKER_00]: The one constitutes an insult... That you get affected by it or not?

549
00:47:29,447 --> 00:47:29,867
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

550
00:47:30,067 --> 00:47:35,533
[SPEAKER_00]: Pain, suffering, unpleasantness, that you want to get rid of.

551
00:47:36,603 --> 00:47:39,025
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to be mindful, you want to be aware.

552
00:47:39,705 --> 00:47:45,369
[SPEAKER_00]: No need for sensations, no sound, nor self-questioning of the psychological nature.

553
00:47:45,610 --> 00:47:47,911
[SPEAKER_00]: All of that will be done inevitably in the beginning.

554
00:47:47,971 --> 00:47:52,615
[SPEAKER_00]: But you need to maintain the sight, like the eyes on the prize, which is... No.

555
00:47:52,895 --> 00:48:02,282
[SPEAKER_00]: Fundamentally, the only reason insult, torture, anything is a problem, is because emotionally it's uncomfortable and I'm habitually resisting it.

556
00:48:02,996 --> 00:48:04,078
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why I suffer.

557
00:48:04,559 --> 00:48:05,440
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because of the words.

558
00:48:05,520 --> 00:48:08,245
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because who hear the words, who is offended.

559
00:48:08,265 --> 00:48:15,457
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because when the experience of disagreeable arises, endures in these five aggregates, there is craving against it.

560
00:48:16,432 --> 00:48:19,553
[SPEAKER_00]: And, without a knowledge of that, it would be impossible.

561
00:48:19,653 --> 00:48:21,513
[SPEAKER_00]: You... a person will never arrive at it.

562
00:48:22,253 --> 00:48:32,755
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter how much management, how much meditation techniques, and vipassanas in the colloquial sense of the word, they do, because it all revolves around being affected already, and missing the point.

563
00:48:33,095 --> 00:48:39,797
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, for... even to the slightest degree, you miss that the point of insult is being unpleasant, and you resist that.

564
00:48:40,417 --> 00:48:41,637
[SPEAKER_00]: To that extent, you will.

565
00:48:42,425 --> 00:48:53,738
[SPEAKER_00]: At least implicitly assume it's the words, it's the person, it's something I've done, I wasn't mindful enough, I didn't do this, I didn't have my meditation, I made all these mistakes, I will not do those mistakes.

566
00:48:53,958 --> 00:49:02,008
[SPEAKER_00]: All of that is secondary to the fact that, no, presence of craving means presence of suffering, not presence of unpleasant words, unpleasant actions, unpleasant thoughts.

567
00:49:03,594 --> 00:49:05,395
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how would you be aware of that?

568
00:49:05,455 --> 00:49:07,975
[SPEAKER_00]: You realize, ah, by recognizing where the problem is.

569
00:49:08,736 --> 00:49:11,256
[SPEAKER_00]: So, mindfulness, knowledge go hand in hand.

570
00:49:11,636 --> 00:49:15,277
[SPEAKER_00]: You will not be able to be mindful of these things, unless you start discerning them.

571
00:49:15,938 --> 00:49:19,839
[SPEAKER_00]: And you will not be able to discern them, unless you actually hear about them, in that sense.

572
00:49:19,939 --> 00:49:20,479
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the...

573
00:49:21,732 --> 00:49:31,157
[SPEAKER_00]: Two factors for the arising of the right view is the right instruction and the right yoniso manasikāra, the proper type of, like, knowing what the attention is, so to speak.

574
00:49:31,698 --> 00:49:40,523
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you just hear my actual words without a peripheral context of your own experience to which these words apply, you have no yoniso basis.

575
00:49:41,948 --> 00:49:47,152
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're just attending, literally, taking word for word, and mantras, and so on, like what people commonly do.

576
00:49:47,712 --> 00:49:49,693
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, this agent said that, that agent said this, and so on.

577
00:49:50,214 --> 00:49:57,699
[SPEAKER_00]: I read the suttas, I study suttas, but, peripherally, you want to discern the context where this applies to you, here and now, this very moment.

578
00:49:58,239 --> 00:50:02,122
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is why, this very moment, the roots of suffering are being maintained by you.

579
00:50:03,585 --> 00:50:05,726
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly how you get even the confidence.

580
00:50:05,746 --> 00:50:09,889
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you're not even afraid of what you will hear in the future.

581
00:50:10,550 --> 00:50:12,411
[SPEAKER_00]: What people will do to you in the future.

582
00:50:13,071 --> 00:50:17,094
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the place where the pain is, has been uprooted.

583
00:50:17,835 --> 00:50:18,735
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is the craving.

584
00:50:19,456 --> 00:50:21,877
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it doesn't really matter what comes this way.

585
00:50:22,878 --> 00:50:24,199
[SPEAKER_00]: The basis of suffering is gone.

586
00:50:24,419 --> 00:50:28,242
[SPEAKER_00]: Because suffering was never in things that were coming through your ears, eyes and so on.

587
00:50:28,622 --> 00:50:30,283
[SPEAKER_00]: Not even through your thoughts, so to speak.

588
00:50:31,688 --> 00:50:41,832
[SPEAKER_00]: It was in your own ignorance of, basically, your own attitude towards your own presently enduring feeling, here and now.

589
00:50:42,352 --> 00:50:43,253
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

590
00:50:43,493 --> 00:50:56,538
[SPEAKER_02]: This point of, if I can paraphrase, generally, yeah, that you suffer, but mainly what makes it suffering, what makes something suffering, is because

591
00:50:57,129 --> 00:50:58,330
[SPEAKER_02]: You... because of craving.

592
00:50:58,350 --> 00:51:05,175
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, what makes anything suffering is because that you, like, want to get rid of it or you want... or want something.

593
00:51:05,876 --> 00:51:07,157
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you're already doing it.

594
00:51:07,177 --> 00:51:07,637
[SPEAKER_00]: So that...

595
00:51:08,098 --> 00:51:09,699
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not, I want to get rid of it.

596
00:51:09,899 --> 00:51:11,481
[SPEAKER_00]: I am already trying to get rid of it.

597
00:51:11,661 --> 00:51:14,723
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why I already suffer.

598
00:51:14,783 --> 00:51:16,825
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not on the level of choosing or not choosing.

599
00:51:17,125 --> 00:51:20,688
[SPEAKER_00]: You're already... if you're already suffering, if you're already affected, you're already getting rid of it.

600
00:51:23,971 --> 00:51:24,191
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

601
00:51:25,322 --> 00:51:52,183
[SPEAKER_02]: And the point where... there was one point that you mentioned, thus... like again... just again in regard to the same example, like hearing... hearing insulting words, thus there is a... because there is... now you mentioned unpleasant feeling arises, has arisen, that you were trying to get rid of, and this is what makes this painful or suffering, if I got it right.

602
00:51:56,304 --> 00:52:12,797
[SPEAKER_02]: But for somebody who's got rid of craving, and got rid of their self-centered point of view, let's say, how does the unpleasant feeling of being insulted arise?

603
00:52:14,839 --> 00:52:16,000
[SPEAKER_02]: How can that be possible?

604
00:52:17,921 --> 00:52:19,843
[SPEAKER_00]: The insult, the disagreeability of it.

605
00:52:21,386 --> 00:52:22,367
[SPEAKER_00]: But they're not resisting it.

606
00:52:22,388 --> 00:52:23,970
[SPEAKER_01]: So it's not... What's disagreeable about this?

607
00:52:24,631 --> 00:52:28,236
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the offensive attitude, the threatening attitude of the person.

608
00:52:28,356 --> 00:52:29,178
[SPEAKER_00]: Perception of that.

609
00:52:29,258 --> 00:52:30,680
[SPEAKER_00]: Perception is disagreeable of it.

610
00:52:32,082 --> 00:52:37,704
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, same when the Buddha said, sometimes a monk asks him for instruction, he gives him the instruction, then he goes and does the opposite.

611
00:52:38,204 --> 00:52:40,924
[SPEAKER_00]: Buddha sees him doing the opposite, not being restrained.

612
00:52:41,425 --> 00:52:43,365
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said, the Tathāgata is disagreed with that.

613
00:52:43,765 --> 00:52:48,366
[SPEAKER_00]: He experienced the disagreeable feeling, but that disagreeable feeling cannot overwhelm his mind.

614
00:52:48,426 --> 00:52:52,328
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, there is no craving in regard to disagreeability of that perception.

615
00:52:53,248 --> 00:52:53,788
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not that...

616
00:52:54,792 --> 00:53:03,700
[SPEAKER_00]: This person who is free from craving and arahant has no concept anymore of people's behavior or this is threatening, this is agreeable, this is disagreeable.

617
00:53:04,120 --> 00:53:04,240
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

618
00:53:04,460 --> 00:53:05,961
[SPEAKER_00]: He cannot be affected by it.

619
00:53:06,782 --> 00:53:10,005
[SPEAKER_00]: But agreeability and disagreeability is still there and that's the difference.

620
00:53:13,667 --> 00:53:19,970
[SPEAKER_02]: But in the sense that, yes, like the... like something is, okay, it's a... Now, I'm shouting at you.

621
00:53:20,130 --> 00:53:24,331
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm shouting, I'm clenching my fists, I'm threatening to hit you, I'm calling you names.

622
00:53:24,371 --> 00:53:25,632
[SPEAKER_00]: All of that is significant.

623
00:53:25,672 --> 00:53:26,412
[SPEAKER_00]: You perceive that.

624
00:53:26,492 --> 00:53:28,573
[SPEAKER_00]: You know what that means.

625
00:53:28,593 --> 00:53:31,034
[SPEAKER_00]: Arahant does not lose the meaning of all these things.

626
00:53:31,554 --> 00:53:33,675
[SPEAKER_00]: But he loses being emotionally overwhelmed by it.

627
00:53:36,159 --> 00:53:37,880
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, you can judge, for example, the behavior.

628
00:53:38,080 --> 00:53:55,672
[SPEAKER_02]: You can see, okay, if you start to shout at me and to threaten me and everything, okay, I can see, okay, he's acting out of anger, it's wrong, like, it's immoral, sort of, like, it's immoral behavior, but why would it be disagreeable for me?

629
00:53:56,292 --> 00:54:01,976
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, the example, or hearing insulting words is, there's no reason for it to be disagreeable for me.

630
00:54:02,211 --> 00:54:04,833
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, there is no reason to not be disagreeable either.

631
00:54:05,553 --> 00:54:08,975
[SPEAKER_00]: You are not in a choice of how things arise for you.

632
00:54:09,595 --> 00:54:11,676
[SPEAKER_00]: You just find things agreeing or disagreeing.

633
00:54:12,277 --> 00:54:22,463
[SPEAKER_00]: Cold, hot... That's why the Buddha compared the words and abuse of others to should be endured like the way you endure the elements, you endure insects biting you.

634
00:54:22,523 --> 00:54:22,763
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

635
00:54:22,943 --> 00:54:30,827
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you trying, why is it that disagreeable, is basically overstepping into the domain of natural perception and trying to say why is it like that.

636
00:54:30,847 --> 00:54:31,628
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter why.

637
00:54:32,975 --> 00:54:37,958
[SPEAKER_00]: The only thing you can know, and you are responsible for, is are you resisting the disagreeable?

638
00:54:39,119 --> 00:54:40,019
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you fearing it?

639
00:54:40,039 --> 00:54:40,980
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you trying to get rid of it?

640
00:54:41,020 --> 00:54:42,721
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you trying to prevent it out of fear and so on?

641
00:54:43,401 --> 00:54:45,222
[SPEAKER_00]: And are you trying to prolong the agreeable?

642
00:54:45,262 --> 00:54:47,203
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you existentially dependent upon agreeable?

643
00:54:47,883 --> 00:54:50,745
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is there disagreeable and agreeable?

644
00:54:50,805 --> 00:54:54,787
[SPEAKER_00]: It's basically like asking why the aggregate of saṅkhara is there?

645
00:54:54,987 --> 00:54:56,748
[SPEAKER_00]: Why the aggregate of perception is there?

646
00:54:57,729 --> 00:55:01,451
[SPEAKER_00]: That's kind of a meaningless question, because the question is the result of the aggregates being there.

647
00:55:05,758 --> 00:55:14,725
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm not sure because in regard to the like disagreeable things that arise from... because, let's say, as... let's talk about...

648
00:55:14,765 --> 00:55:29,036
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, a sound can be... or like a... yeah, a sound can be something that like physically is... yeah, hearing a sound can be sort of physically painful for the ear, like I say, a really loud sound.

649
00:55:29,296 --> 00:55:29,516
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, sure.

650
00:55:29,536 --> 00:55:30,237
[SPEAKER_02]: This is sort of a...

651
00:55:30,457 --> 00:55:31,358
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's disagreeable.

652
00:55:32,549 --> 00:55:37,852
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't suffer, you don't fear it, but it's disagreeable in itself.

653
00:55:37,872 --> 00:55:38,132
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

654
00:55:38,952 --> 00:55:40,453
[SPEAKER_02]: It's felt as disagreeable.

655
00:55:40,533 --> 00:55:44,335
[SPEAKER_00]: So, asking why is it disagreeable, it's like asking why is there a sound.

656
00:55:44,775 --> 00:55:46,436
[SPEAKER_00]: It's on the same footing.

657
00:55:47,977 --> 00:55:56,241
[SPEAKER_02]: But what I was going to say is that the... like, disagreeable from hearing words is a bit different, because that's not something physical.

658
00:55:57,142 --> 00:55:59,723
[SPEAKER_02]: It's only on the level of how the mind

659
00:56:00,602 --> 00:56:10,351
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, there would be no...

660
00:56:10,691 --> 00:56:10,931
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

661
00:56:11,712 --> 00:56:13,294
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, see, it's not the words.

662
00:56:13,594 --> 00:56:15,716
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's the context that you're still hearing.

663
00:56:15,816 --> 00:56:20,920
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't hear the movie, the mind will have no context of impersonal insult.

664
00:56:21,661 --> 00:56:26,826
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whichever way your mind takes it, it did... it boils down to the sound you heard.

665
00:56:27,947 --> 00:56:29,048
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, that's what the Buddha said.

666
00:56:29,108 --> 00:56:35,090
[SPEAKER_00]: He... a skillful monk can use heat, cold, insect bites, abuse of others, and so on.

667
00:56:35,190 --> 00:56:37,251
[SPEAKER_00]: It's on the level of elements besetting you.

668
00:56:38,412 --> 00:56:41,333
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there is meaning even in meaningless sounds, so to speak.

669
00:56:41,433 --> 00:56:43,514
[SPEAKER_00]: Meaning to the point of disagreeability.

670
00:56:44,174 --> 00:56:45,755
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what it means, screeching sound.

671
00:56:46,815 --> 00:56:47,496
[SPEAKER_00]: Agreeability.

672
00:56:47,516 --> 00:56:48,876
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't... it's nothing specific.

673
00:56:48,936 --> 00:56:50,097
[SPEAKER_00]: There are no words to it.

674
00:56:51,032 --> 00:56:55,034
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's still, in its very essence, it suits or it disturbs.

675
00:56:55,454 --> 00:56:55,834
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

676
00:56:56,575 --> 00:56:57,615
[SPEAKER_00]: And same with the speech.

677
00:56:58,096 --> 00:57:03,458
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, speech gets more elaborate because now there's all layers of meaning of the words and the language and designations.

678
00:57:04,199 --> 00:57:07,900
[SPEAKER_00]: But fundamentally, none of that would exist if somebody didn't actually say it.

679
00:57:08,701 --> 00:57:09,841
[SPEAKER_00]: If you didn't hear it.

680
00:57:11,102 --> 00:57:13,103
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the sound is there still as a basis.

681
00:57:14,828 --> 00:57:20,291
[SPEAKER_00]: And fundamentally, it doesn't matter how much it means, positively or negatively, what the person said.

682
00:57:20,831 --> 00:57:24,614
[SPEAKER_00]: Fundamentally, it all remains bound with the sound that you heard, the words.

683
00:57:24,734 --> 00:57:27,275
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's agreeable or disagreeable, fundamentally.

684
00:57:29,116 --> 00:57:30,377
[SPEAKER_00]: Pleasing or displeasing?

685
00:57:34,819 --> 00:57:41,403
[SPEAKER_02]: Just the point I was trying to make, maybe it doesn't matter, but that the disagreeable is not in the sound.

686
00:57:41,443 --> 00:57:44,425
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not because of the nature of the sound.

687
00:57:45,139 --> 00:57:50,483
[SPEAKER_02]: It's only because of the nature of the meaning, if it's about words... That's where you're suffering.

688
00:57:50,523 --> 00:57:55,107
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the difference between suffering on account of disagreeable and disagreeable I'm talking about.

689
00:57:56,207 --> 00:58:00,070
[SPEAKER_00]: And the disagreeable I'm talking about is in the sound that you heard.

690
00:58:00,130 --> 00:58:02,432
[SPEAKER_00]: That's as far as basically your responsibility goes.

691
00:58:03,953 --> 00:58:12,880
[SPEAKER_00]: You can now overanalyze and say it's because they meant this or they meant that, but fundamentally none of that would be even there if you haven't heard it.

692
00:58:15,821 --> 00:58:16,701
[SPEAKER_00]: It would not be relevant.

693
00:58:17,162 --> 00:58:18,542
[SPEAKER_00]: It would be like a movie you didn't watch.

694
00:58:30,567 --> 00:58:43,493
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's quite important what I'm saying is because if you do divorce agreeability and disagreeability from the perception through your senses, it enables you then to go through the analysis of the sound

695
00:58:44,587 --> 00:58:53,095
[SPEAKER_00]: Not in an abstract sense, but like, who, where it came from, separate the meaning, which kind of basically feels almost as if you are dealing with a problem of it.

696
00:58:54,276 --> 00:58:54,696
[SPEAKER_00]: But you're not.

697
00:58:55,196 --> 00:58:59,620
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is not that sound is agreeable or disagreeable or neither, maybe it's a neutral sound.

698
00:59:00,241 --> 00:59:07,047
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is that agreeability, disagreeability, neutrality, means basis for craving for you within you.

699
00:59:09,825 --> 00:59:19,739
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you if you're focusing on the relationship between the sound and disagreeability, and why is it disagreeable, or why it shouldn't be, and the meaning and so on, means you're not dealing where the problem is.

700
00:59:20,339 --> 00:59:21,841
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it doesn't matter why it's disagreeable.

701
00:59:22,322 --> 00:59:25,146
[SPEAKER_00]: What matters is, is there craving against the disagreeable?

702
00:59:26,257 --> 00:59:29,979
[SPEAKER_00]: Experience as a whole of somebody saying mean words to me.

703
00:59:29,999 --> 00:59:34,142
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter the individual syllables, the words, the accent, none of that is relevant.

704
00:59:34,522 --> 00:59:35,783
[SPEAKER_00]: Who they are is irrelevant.

705
00:59:36,003 --> 00:59:37,044
[SPEAKER_00]: Why did they say this?

706
00:59:37,124 --> 00:59:38,104
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do they want to hurt me?

707
00:59:39,065 --> 00:59:39,765
[SPEAKER_00]: Absolutely irrelevant.

708
00:59:39,845 --> 00:59:44,028
[SPEAKER_00]: What relevant is, the experience as a whole is disagreeable, and that's what I'm resisting.

709
00:59:44,108 --> 00:59:48,771
[SPEAKER_00]: If I stop resisting the disagreeability, the actual violence cannot harm me anymore.

710
00:59:51,047 --> 00:59:57,829
[SPEAKER_02]: So, how do you stop... what... like, what do you mean by resist... stopping to resist the disagreeable of it?

711
00:59:57,929 --> 00:59:58,429
[SPEAKER_00]: How do you do that?

712
00:59:58,449 --> 00:59:59,669
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, first see... first you need to see.

713
00:59:59,789 --> 01:00:00,069
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

714
01:00:00,109 --> 01:00:04,571
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you think disagreeability is not in the perception, you have misconceived it.

715
01:00:05,671 --> 01:00:09,872
[SPEAKER_00]: If you think disagreeability is... equals your suffering, you have misconceived it.

716
01:00:10,896 --> 01:00:20,584
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to see disagreeability of the experience as a whole, as a risen phenomena, that you did not necessarily create, and you have no say in it directly, but you are subjected to it.

717
01:00:21,365 --> 01:00:24,888
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why sense restraint needs to be done beforehand.

718
01:00:24,968 --> 01:00:34,576
[SPEAKER_00]: Sense restraint that prevents one's mind from blaming the sound, or the person they said, or something, or attacking them, implying they are the cause for my suffering.

719
01:00:35,197 --> 01:00:38,519
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you stop, you start restraining, then you still

720
01:00:39,360 --> 01:00:40,821
[SPEAKER_00]: Try to analyze the disagreeable.

721
01:00:41,001 --> 01:00:41,841
[SPEAKER_00]: Why did I say this?

722
01:00:41,941 --> 01:00:43,001
[SPEAKER_00]: Why did I react like that?

723
01:00:43,081 --> 01:00:43,802
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe I shouldn't do it.

724
01:00:44,122 --> 01:00:45,002
[SPEAKER_00]: Was I mindful enough?

725
01:00:45,162 --> 01:00:49,463
[SPEAKER_00]: All of that revolves around the fact that you are resisting the disagreeable, which means you're not seeing it.

726
01:00:50,224 --> 01:00:52,585
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not seeing it for what it is enduring there.

727
01:00:54,345 --> 01:01:01,267
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you'd start, actually, if you stop investing effort in managing the disagreeable, it's like, no, disagreeability is present.

728
01:01:01,688 --> 01:01:02,848
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter on account of what.

729
01:01:03,248 --> 01:01:05,309
[SPEAKER_00]: And then refrain for as long as it takes.

730
01:01:06,607 --> 01:01:15,893
[SPEAKER_00]: From jumping into the management of the disagreeable, then you get to see that all you have is disagreeability or agreeability and your attitude towards it.

731
01:01:16,154 --> 01:01:17,214
[SPEAKER_00]: That's basically the core.

732
01:01:17,234 --> 01:01:18,435
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the only relevance here.

733
01:01:19,116 --> 01:01:23,439
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the gateway for any suffering, for any lust, for any aversion that will then be manifested.

734
01:01:25,340 --> 01:01:27,041
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you welcoming the agreeable?

735
01:01:27,982 --> 01:01:32,365
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you resisting the disagreeable as a pre-emptive attitude, pretty much, as a view?

736
01:01:33,567 --> 01:01:40,388
[SPEAKER_00]: And the only way you can see the view in regard to agreeability or disagreeability is to first start seeing agreeability or disagreeability correctly.

737
01:01:41,229 --> 01:01:45,750
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Buddha himself said, all I'm teaching is suffering, like, correctly.

738
01:01:45,890 --> 01:01:47,970
[SPEAKER_00]: People don't even know what suffering is.

739
01:01:48,010 --> 01:01:50,290
[SPEAKER_00]: All I'm teaching is dukkha and freedom from...

740
01:01:50,330 --> 01:01:53,751
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'm actually teaching you dukkha, like the Buddha would say.

741
01:01:54,871 --> 01:02:02,193
[SPEAKER_00]: Means people, like, can suffer the utmost, the greatest misfortune and still completely have no idea what the actual dukkha is.

742
01:02:03,557 --> 01:02:05,098
[SPEAKER_00]: So, dukkha and freedom from dukkha.

743
01:02:05,538 --> 01:02:07,439
[SPEAKER_00]: Agreeability and freedom from agreeability.

744
01:02:07,579 --> 01:02:09,720
[SPEAKER_00]: Disagreeability, freedom from disagreeability.

745
01:02:10,440 --> 01:02:14,081
[SPEAKER_00]: But it needs to be seen for what it is.

746
01:02:23,565 --> 01:02:28,747
[SPEAKER_00]: Because even when you have the view that... but disagreeability doesn't feel like it's in the words.

747
01:02:29,168 --> 01:02:29,668
[SPEAKER_00]: All of that

748
01:02:30,725 --> 01:02:37,311
[SPEAKER_00]: You have pretty much developed, inferred, maintained, on account of experiencing the disagreeable words first.

749
01:02:38,392 --> 01:02:46,399
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, your mind, your thinking, took you into explaining that it's not in the words and so on, because it was first, and it remained first.

750
01:02:46,419 --> 01:02:47,540
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you realize, it doesn't matter.

751
01:02:48,427 --> 01:02:50,168
[SPEAKER_00]: As I said, whether it's in this...

752
01:02:50,188 --> 01:02:57,073
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this point is, experience as a whole of certain words, in this particular instance, felt disagreeable and it was a problem for me.

753
01:02:57,734 --> 01:03:04,118
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why you gave in to all these reflections, basically revolving around trying to... to get rid of it, trying to free yourself from it.

754
01:03:06,280 --> 01:03:14,966
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, but I agree, like, this is not... this is useless, like, to kind of analyze after the event is... like, you're too late, like, that you've already... you've already

755
01:03:15,815 --> 01:03:23,277
[SPEAKER_02]: It was disagreeable, it was already a problem, and now you say, ah, it was only because of the... it was only... it was only because of the meaning, it was not because of the words.

756
01:03:23,297 --> 01:03:25,958
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, well, that is useless.

757
01:03:28,239 --> 01:03:30,199
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, this, eh... say agreed.

758
01:03:30,780 --> 01:03:43,843
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, eh...

759
01:03:50,322 --> 01:04:11,088
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, a bit of a related point, what if... because now we're talking about agreeable and disagreeable, but in... and, like, feeling, feeling suffering on account of that, but what I... like, I would say that also, like, you know, you can talk about it on that level, but I mean...

760
01:04:13,457 --> 01:04:18,082
[SPEAKER_02]: There is the kind of additional problem, let's say, in getting angry about something.

761
01:04:19,163 --> 01:04:33,937
[SPEAKER_02]: One is that it's disagreeable, but I think most people, like as a normal person, when you get angry, you don't even kind of notice that it's disagreeable, because you're so focusing on the object of it, the anger itself, you don't see it as disagreeable.

762
01:04:35,577 --> 01:04:44,585
[SPEAKER_02]: The problem with anger is more that it's unwholesome, that it's wrong, that it's not good.

763
01:04:45,325 --> 01:04:58,436
[SPEAKER_02]: So, from that point of view, you need to learn to not get angry in the first place.

764
01:05:15,088 --> 01:05:38,915
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, if you kind of... if you are getting... if you are getting angry already, then... like, if you're getting angry and then kind of saying it's not... if you... if you allow yourself to get angry and to continue to get angry and to speak out of anger, like, and your aim... yeah, your aim can be to not get angry in the first place, but I would say you kind of have to start by first not allowing yourself to... not allowing the anger to continue.

765
01:05:39,656 --> 01:05:40,816
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's... yeah, sure.

766
01:05:40,836 --> 01:05:43,717
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why I say you need to start restraining yourself first.

767
01:05:45,831 --> 01:05:51,155
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not going to see things on the level of your mind if your physical and verbal actions are not restrained beforehand.

768
01:05:51,195 --> 01:05:58,960
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have to stop acting out of anger, you have to stop acting out of lust, with a goal of not being affected by it in the first place.

769
01:06:00,882 --> 01:06:09,408
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you're still affected by it in the first place, not acting, well restrained, but affected in the first place, you still have a massive problem there.

770
01:06:11,129 --> 01:06:11,810
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the root.

771
01:06:17,070 --> 01:06:21,834
[SPEAKER_00]: And it doesn't matter if you're getting affected as much or I'm not getting affected as much.

772
01:06:21,974 --> 01:06:22,554
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

773
01:06:22,594 --> 01:06:25,717
[SPEAKER_00]: Nature of being affected is something I'm still susceptible to.

774
01:06:26,237 --> 01:06:28,039
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, it's still the massive problem.

775
01:06:28,359 --> 01:06:34,144
[SPEAKER_00]: Because often, what happens when people get into practice, they start cultivating the environment that suits them, which is fine.

776
01:06:34,624 --> 01:06:37,687
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, they start to depend on that environment.

777
01:06:38,207 --> 01:06:40,368
[SPEAKER_00]: Whereby, you will have a protected environment.

778
01:06:40,388 --> 01:06:42,108
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you might be the senior monk.

779
01:06:42,208 --> 01:06:45,649
[SPEAKER_00]: Nobody will be challenging you, offending you, questioning you.

780
01:06:46,110 --> 01:06:47,790
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing really to poke you around.

781
01:06:48,190 --> 01:06:52,912
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't really have this anger arising anymore, because now you're basically the lord of the land.

782
01:06:53,732 --> 01:06:55,533
[SPEAKER_00]: Everybody is your servant, so to speak.

783
01:06:55,613 --> 01:06:57,293
[SPEAKER_00]: So, but that doesn't mean you dealt with it.

784
01:06:57,873 --> 01:06:59,154
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why the Buddha would say...

785
01:06:59,174 --> 01:07:05,356
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you then ask yourself, okay, I'm not experiencing any current anger, but can the unarisen anger arise for me?

786
01:07:06,365 --> 01:07:09,287
[SPEAKER_00]: If you have no idea, means yes, you got your answer.

787
01:07:10,028 --> 01:07:17,494
[SPEAKER_00]: So, and that should be regarded as the root of the problem, not the fact that most of my time I'm not lustful, most of my time I'm not angry.

788
01:07:17,914 --> 01:07:20,616
[SPEAKER_00]: That's because of the circumstances or because you're an arahant.

789
01:07:20,696 --> 01:07:21,377
[SPEAKER_00]: Which one is it?

790
01:07:23,258 --> 01:07:25,000
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if a person is honest, they'll know the answer.

791
01:07:26,541 --> 01:07:26,821
[SPEAKER_00]: But no.

792
01:07:26,901 --> 01:07:29,664
[SPEAKER_00]: Often, often it's regarded as, oh, dhamma helps me.

793
01:07:29,764 --> 01:07:31,165
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't get angry as much.

794
01:07:32,065 --> 01:07:33,347
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you still liable to anger?

795
01:07:33,407 --> 01:07:33,647
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

796
01:07:34,027 --> 01:07:34,828
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not the dhamma.

797
01:07:34,868 --> 01:07:38,471
[SPEAKER_00]: That's just your management on account of how you took dhamma to be.

798
01:07:38,711 --> 01:07:40,172
[SPEAKER_00]: But dhamma actually will uproot it.

799
01:07:41,893 --> 01:07:43,695
[SPEAKER_00]: Means you will be incapable of anger.

800
01:07:49,022 --> 01:07:55,545
[SPEAKER_02]: It reminded me of that story with the mistress Veda Hika, I think, and the one, the maid servant.

801
01:07:55,565 --> 01:08:00,827
[SPEAKER_02]: The mistress was supposed to be so patient and so kind and so calm.

802
01:08:00,867 --> 01:08:01,827
[SPEAKER_00]: She was testing her, yeah.

803
01:08:01,847 --> 01:08:02,867
[SPEAKER_02]: Maid servant, yeah.

804
01:08:03,127 --> 01:08:04,208
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm a tester.

805
01:08:05,088 --> 01:08:10,070
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

806
01:08:14,372 --> 01:08:14,912
[SPEAKER_02]: So...

807
01:08:19,727 --> 01:08:21,067
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a good point.

808
01:08:21,107 --> 01:08:21,928
[SPEAKER_02]: It's true like this.

809
01:08:22,028 --> 01:08:30,512
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, I was wondering even... because I'm not a monk and I don't... or a nun and we don't live like that.

810
01:08:32,133 --> 01:08:43,619
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's kind of interesting way of practicing here that you have kind of plenty of opportunity to see that, like,

811
01:08:45,802 --> 01:08:49,966
[SPEAKER_02]: Anger can arise, or like the... that these... that these things can arise.

812
01:08:49,986 --> 01:08:55,230
[SPEAKER_02]: You're not in sort of a position where you... when you're never being confronted by anybody.

813
01:08:55,310 --> 01:09:02,336
[SPEAKER_02]: So, in one sense, I would say, that's almost a useful thing, because you have to kind of be confronted by the... by the fact that...

814
01:09:02,416 --> 01:09:03,317
[SPEAKER_00]: In a way, exactly.

815
01:09:03,657 --> 01:09:06,319
[SPEAKER_00]: I think if... it can be... it can be beneficial for people.

816
01:09:06,359 --> 01:09:13,125
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's... again, that's another place for peripheral awareness, whichever... whichever way we want to call it, would be handy, would... would actually

817
01:09:14,210 --> 01:09:15,612
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, result in knowledge.

818
01:09:16,092 --> 01:09:20,938
[SPEAKER_00]: If people's practice is about current sensations and present moment, present moment, present moment...

819
01:09:21,198 --> 01:09:22,279
[SPEAKER_00]: Presently, I have no lust.

820
01:09:22,359 --> 01:09:23,421
[SPEAKER_00]: Presently, I have no ill will.

821
01:09:23,761 --> 01:09:24,141
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

822
01:09:24,302 --> 01:09:25,183
[SPEAKER_00]: That's my concern.

823
01:09:25,263 --> 01:09:25,963
[SPEAKER_00]: Present moment.

824
01:09:26,564 --> 01:09:26,784
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

825
01:09:27,165 --> 01:09:29,047
[SPEAKER_00]: Future present moment should be your concern.

826
01:09:29,708 --> 01:09:31,450
[SPEAKER_00]: Unarisen lust, can it arise?

827
01:09:32,410 --> 01:09:45,636
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, that's another present moment that I'm obviously ignoring because I'm so absorbed in present sensations and present breathing and presently being here and now, you know, judgment-free or whatever else, you know, people psychologize.

828
01:09:46,656 --> 01:09:47,476
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, that's fine.

829
01:09:47,556 --> 01:09:47,797
[SPEAKER_00]: Great.

830
01:09:47,897 --> 01:09:49,497
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the environment is suitable.

831
01:09:49,817 --> 01:09:50,878
[SPEAKER_00]: You made some effort.

832
01:09:51,438 --> 01:09:52,999
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you liable to lust?

833
01:09:53,339 --> 01:09:54,820
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you liable to aversion?

834
01:09:55,520 --> 01:09:55,620
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh,

835
01:09:56,583 --> 01:09:59,810
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you are, you are presently liable, ironically enough.

836
01:10:00,231 --> 01:10:07,166
[SPEAKER_00]: You're so absorbed in the present moment, that you don't see your present liability to the very things you think you're overcoming.

837
01:10:10,686 --> 01:10:15,570
[SPEAKER_00]: See, the possibility of future lust, it's not present there, so you cannot attend to it.

838
01:10:15,590 --> 01:10:24,337
[SPEAKER_00]: That's exactly why people have... people who are used to this kind of practice of just... just what's in front, have problem understanding what yoniso manasikāra is, the way we describe it.

839
01:10:24,377 --> 01:10:29,421
[SPEAKER_00]: Have... cannot grasp peripheral, because if the moment they think it, they make it in the front.

840
01:10:30,041 --> 01:10:30,642
[SPEAKER_00]: But it is there.

841
01:10:30,802 --> 01:10:32,383
[SPEAKER_00]: A possibility of future lust.

842
01:10:32,463 --> 01:10:33,124
[SPEAKER_00]: It's exactly that.

843
01:10:33,644 --> 01:10:37,627
[SPEAKER_00]: See, it's an intention that persists there, because you're not free from lust.

844
01:10:38,488 --> 01:10:49,437
[SPEAKER_00]: Arahant has no possibility of future lust, no intentions of lust, gone, uprooted, because he has made the effort on that right level, understood it.

845
01:10:54,040 --> 01:10:59,264
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, but yeah, this was a point in the video yesterday that I wanted to ask about anyway.

846
01:10:59,284 --> 01:11:07,051
[SPEAKER_02]: So, like, when you say the possibility of future, like, the possibility of future

847
01:11:08,193 --> 01:11:16,621
[SPEAKER_02]: A possibility of future lust means its current liability.

848
01:11:16,721 --> 01:11:17,742
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's a problem.

849
01:11:30,040 --> 01:11:31,841
[SPEAKER_02]: It basically means that you haven't uprooted it.

850
01:11:31,961 --> 01:11:35,643
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, as long as it's there because you have not uprooted it.

851
01:11:35,923 --> 01:11:36,323
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

852
01:11:36,343 --> 01:11:39,104
[SPEAKER_00]: It means you haven't actually, yeah, you haven't fixed the problem.

853
01:11:39,184 --> 01:11:48,288
[SPEAKER_00]: So, everything else that's around you, like, oh, most of the time I'm not angry, most of the time I'm not lustful, that's all circumstantial and should be regarded as such for as long as you don't uproot it.

854
01:11:48,808 --> 01:11:49,809
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the true freedom.

855
01:11:51,149 --> 01:11:57,032
[SPEAKER_00]: But many people, by just focusing on what's in front, what's in front is that I don't get lustful, don't get angry.

856
01:11:57,612 --> 01:11:57,772
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I

857
01:11:59,983 --> 01:12:04,967
[SPEAKER_00]: But until you get lustful or angry, then you quickly deal with it, and then you say, well, most of the time I'm fine.

858
01:12:07,289 --> 01:12:11,792
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you're honest, you would see, being liable to it, that's where the problem is rooted.

859
01:12:11,852 --> 01:12:13,173
[SPEAKER_00]: So, am I free from that or not?

860
01:12:13,633 --> 01:12:17,777
[SPEAKER_00]: If not, means most of the time circumstances suit me and they don't challenge me.

861
01:12:17,917 --> 01:12:23,461
[SPEAKER_00]: If that were to change, my mind would be overwhelmed by those things that I basically think I uprooted.

862
01:12:27,473 --> 01:12:31,614
[SPEAKER_00]: And you don't want to wait for that to tell you, to happen, because it would be too late to deal with it.

863
01:12:33,855 --> 01:12:34,095
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

864
01:12:34,815 --> 01:12:49,558
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, like, yeah, I see that it's, like, it's, it's a useful, it can be, like, it's a useful thing to, like, ask yourself or to put this kind of pressure on yourself to, eh... say, okay, even if I'm not getting angry right now, I can get angry or could I...

865
01:12:49,599 --> 01:12:50,299
[SPEAKER_02]: It's necessary.

866
01:12:50,339 --> 01:12:51,419
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not, it's not optional.

867
01:12:51,459 --> 01:12:52,819
[SPEAKER_00]: It's completely, it's absolutely...

868
01:12:52,859 --> 01:12:55,140
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the only gateway towards right uprooting.

869
01:12:55,950 --> 01:12:57,051
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything else is management.

870
01:12:57,811 --> 01:13:11,380
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if all you do is read the suttas and practice, unless it's done through this gateway of liability to dukkha, one way or the other, it's going to be on the level of management.

871
01:13:12,381 --> 01:13:23,348
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, but the question I had was about, like, why do you... because, I mean, is dukkha not present anyway

872
01:13:23,938 --> 01:13:31,862
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, in your current experience, if you are like... because it's present... is it not present anyway?

873
01:13:32,742 --> 01:13:35,844
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, why do you need to think about the liability for it in the future?

874
01:13:35,924 --> 01:13:38,885
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's what dukkha is.

875
01:13:38,905 --> 01:13:41,106
[SPEAKER_00]: Dukkha, uneasiness, dis-ease.

876
01:13:41,506 --> 01:13:42,106
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what it is.

877
01:13:42,287 --> 01:13:42,887
[SPEAKER_00]: Liability.

878
01:13:43,907 --> 01:13:48,349
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, the divine messenger, the Buddha, wasn't sick, wasn't old and wasn't dead.

879
01:13:49,610 --> 01:13:51,291
[SPEAKER_00]: But he recognizes inevitability.

880
01:13:51,983 --> 01:13:56,245
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if he just focused on whether it was present, he wouldn't have seen those things as divine messages.

881
01:13:56,626 --> 01:14:00,648
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need even wisdom to recognize where the problem is, let alone to begin dealing with it.

882
01:14:04,818 --> 01:14:15,128
[SPEAKER_00]: The fact that you're liable to suffering, if you recognize the liability currently, it's currently unpleasant.

883
01:14:15,648 --> 01:14:20,272
[SPEAKER_00]: So, actually, dukkha is present for everyone who is not free from it, from that liability.

884
01:14:20,392 --> 01:14:21,433
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just that it's not seen.

885
01:14:21,934 --> 01:14:25,537
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, the Buddha teaches dukkha first and then freedom from it.

886
01:14:26,758 --> 01:14:28,920
[SPEAKER_00]: Most people won't even see that liability as a problem.

887
01:14:32,273 --> 01:14:43,960
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, yeah, like pay... when you pay attention to the idea that you are... that you're subject to suffering, this is unpleasant, or this can be... this can be unpleasant.

888
01:14:44,881 --> 01:14:47,463
[SPEAKER_02]: That is... it is unpleasant.

889
01:14:49,604 --> 01:15:01,832
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you don't pay attention to the idea of the fact that you can... that you will suffer, that you will get old, that you will get sick, that you will die, you don't pay attention to that, then you don't feel that is suffering.

890
01:15:02,375 --> 01:15:04,436
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's where you need to start paying attention to it.

891
01:15:05,137 --> 01:15:06,338
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it is your reality.

892
01:15:06,438 --> 01:15:07,158
[SPEAKER_00]: It's unavoidable.

893
01:15:07,678 --> 01:15:14,583
[SPEAKER_00]: You can choose to not pay attention to things that are avoidable, but it's an unwise thing to not pay attention to things that are unavoidable.

894
01:15:15,343 --> 01:15:16,324
[SPEAKER_00]: The divine messengers.

895
01:15:16,944 --> 01:15:22,127
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, yeah, if you don't pay attention to sight, sound, smell, taste, touches that bother you, they'll stop bothering you.

896
01:15:22,488 --> 01:15:25,609
[SPEAKER_00]: The fact is, you can be bothered and you will be bothered first.

897
01:15:26,350 --> 01:15:29,492
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you choosing to not pay attention is you choosing to ignore the problem.

898
01:15:31,076 --> 01:15:34,618
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, if you if you don't accept that you are ill, you'll think you're healthy.

899
01:15:36,580 --> 01:15:36,840
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

900
01:15:38,881 --> 01:15:43,004
[SPEAKER_00]: And that is pretty much the common world's sort of way of dealing with the problem.

901
01:15:43,825 --> 01:15:44,485
[SPEAKER_00]: Just ignore it.

902
01:15:44,705 --> 01:15:45,506
[SPEAKER_00]: Just ignore the problem.

903
01:15:45,886 --> 01:15:46,387
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not there.

904
01:15:46,687 --> 01:15:47,607
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't pay attention to it.

905
01:15:48,628 --> 01:15:52,891
[SPEAKER_00]: Absorb yourself with your nostrils or soles of feet or where the chair touches you or something.

906
01:15:53,411 --> 01:15:58,615
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't pay attention to your current liability of your existence, because it's just too overwhelming.

907
01:16:03,320 --> 01:16:06,643
[SPEAKER_02]: And what do you mean exactly by, like, paying attention...

908
01:16:06,663 --> 01:16:10,327
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, you have the... you can have the idea that I will suffer in the future.

909
01:16:10,347 --> 01:16:21,557
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, so, like, then... and, like, I remember you, eh... and said in the video yesterday, like, you don't need to keep... you don't need to keep paying attention to the idea and keep paying attention to the idea that you will suffer in the future.

910
01:16:21,577 --> 01:16:27,943
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you need to pay... keep paying attention to that idea if... if the mind is liable to, like, just ignore the problem.

911
01:16:28,752 --> 01:16:40,041
[SPEAKER_00]: But once you become aware of the problem, you start feeling that concern, that dukkha, then you don't have to keep paying attention to it in the same sense of, oh, am I li... because now you feel it that you are liable and you already know.

912
01:16:40,161 --> 01:16:45,705
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what you feel becomes pretty much the exemplification of your knowledge of your situation.

913
01:16:46,746 --> 01:16:47,726
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly what I said.

914
01:16:47,746 --> 01:16:52,030
[SPEAKER_00]: If a person does feel dukkha correctly, there are only two possible outcomes.

915
01:16:53,151 --> 01:16:54,772
[SPEAKER_00]: Madness or noble search.

916
01:16:55,853 --> 01:16:57,333
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no third possibility.

917
01:16:57,954 --> 01:17:06,016
[SPEAKER_00]: If dukkha is seen correctly, mind will either go mad if it cannot deal with it, or if it has enough composure, it will seek, how do I uproot this?

918
01:17:07,577 --> 01:17:13,038
[SPEAKER_00]: And most people never even allow that choice to manifest, even the ones that commit to the practice of dhamma and so on.

919
01:17:13,999 --> 01:17:23,062
[SPEAKER_00]: It's all done on the level of management of the current disturbances, not on the level of uprooting my fundamental liability.

920
01:17:23,082 --> 01:17:24,422
[SPEAKER_00]: Sickness, aging, death.

921
01:17:52,280 --> 01:17:59,565
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I'm just trying to... Like, yeah.

922
01:17:59,585 --> 01:18:05,630
[SPEAKER_02]: So, in the beginning, you... from the beginning, you need to recognize that you're... that... that, yeah, that you...

923
01:18:07,138 --> 01:18:15,620
[SPEAKER_02]: You need to recognize, yeah, this is like to get the... to start practicing from the beginning with the right attitude.

924
01:18:16,140 --> 01:18:16,660
[SPEAKER_02]: It should be this.

925
01:18:16,680 --> 01:18:19,801
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you have to know that you are sick in order to go see the doctor.

926
01:18:20,481 --> 01:18:27,883
[SPEAKER_02]: Otherwise, yeah, if you don't know that you are sick, if you don't feel that you are sick, you don't go to see the doctor.

927
01:18:30,944 --> 01:18:35,085
[SPEAKER_02]: But, like, what is still not that clear for me is, like, the...

928
01:18:45,237 --> 01:18:48,858
[SPEAKER_02]: Because then, I mean, then you start to... then you have to start to practice, right?

929
01:18:48,878 --> 01:18:52,779
[SPEAKER_02]: So, like, what I'm trying to understand is what... What the practice is.

930
01:18:52,799 --> 01:18:55,739
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, what is the... what the practice is.

931
01:18:56,379 --> 01:18:57,560
[SPEAKER_02]: It's... yeah.

932
01:18:57,700 --> 01:18:58,300
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, that's the thing.

933
01:18:58,360 --> 01:18:59,520
[SPEAKER_00]: If you start living...

934
01:18:59,900 --> 01:19:13,563
[SPEAKER_00]: If you start living within the recognition of your pretty much perpetual liability to dukkha, pray to suffering, as we would say, and not give in to, like, psychologizing, managing, trying to explain it to yourself so you don't suffer as much,

935
01:19:15,058 --> 01:19:19,662
[SPEAKER_00]: You will then abandon that which is basically liable to that suffering.

936
01:19:19,762 --> 01:19:27,428
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you would then... and that's where this... all the other talks we did on the sense of ownership, anicca seen correctly, that's where it applies.

937
01:19:28,271 --> 01:19:33,395
[SPEAKER_00]: Not on the level of explanation of the world as of, oh, yes, the modern science has said everything is impermanent.

938
01:19:33,415 --> 01:19:36,257
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha has said, see, modern science and dhamma, it's the same.

939
01:19:36,337 --> 01:19:36,517
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

940
01:19:37,277 --> 01:19:44,823
[SPEAKER_00]: First, you need to feel that existential liability and then recognize that anicca and then give up that because of which you suffer.

941
01:19:45,623 --> 01:19:50,647
[SPEAKER_00]: Give up your self-centeredness, give up the dependence on pleasure, all the upādānas the Buddha talked about.

942
01:19:51,548 --> 01:19:57,532
[SPEAKER_00]: But only on the basis of that framework of I am liable and this needs to be uprooted.

943
01:19:59,760 --> 01:20:10,286
[SPEAKER_00]: Because otherwise, if you take it, if you don't take it on that basis, sorry, if you don't take it on that basis, it will be taken through the attitude of management of the disturbance of suffering, not the uprooting of the liability to it.

944
01:20:24,273 --> 01:20:27,655
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, I mean, like, what I was talking about was more about the

945
01:20:30,606 --> 01:20:31,106
[SPEAKER_02]: I agree.

946
01:20:31,166 --> 01:20:37,788
[SPEAKER_02]: You're trying to just managing and not have things that are disagreeable to you.

947
01:20:38,208 --> 01:20:42,449
[SPEAKER_02]: Not to suffer so much.

948
01:20:43,169 --> 01:20:43,750
[SPEAKER_02]: It's no use.

949
01:20:43,770 --> 01:20:51,332
[SPEAKER_02]: For example, you will always just try to get away from the things that are disagreeable, but you won't try to remove the things that are agreeable.

950
01:20:52,012 --> 01:20:58,054
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the management I'm referring to, it's not necessarily... it's not like a choice or people choose, I will manage.

951
01:20:58,758 --> 01:21:08,440
[SPEAKER_00]: It's basically the default result if you're not operating within the recognition of liability to suffering.

952
01:21:09,601 --> 01:21:16,442
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're not operating within the framework of that existential anxiety, you are managing your suffering, even unknowingly.

953
01:21:16,522 --> 01:21:27,445
[SPEAKER_00]: Things you do will be on the level of management, not uprooting it, because you have, well, either have no recognition or have fallen from the recognition of that general anxious nature of your situation.

954
01:21:28,795 --> 01:21:30,300
[SPEAKER_00]: dukkha, unease, disease.

955
01:21:32,206 --> 01:21:33,891
[SPEAKER_00]: Not dukkha, oh, this is bothering me.

956
01:21:34,413 --> 01:21:35,577
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all secondary to it.

957
01:21:47,436 --> 01:21:51,818
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, because, I mean, dukkha is... if it's only when something is bothering you, then it's very easy.

958
01:21:51,858 --> 01:21:56,919
[SPEAKER_02]: You can just try to find people to put yourself in a place where it doesn't bother you.

959
01:21:57,039 --> 01:22:02,121
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is not that people haven't heard enough of dhamma or haven't read enough of the suttas.

960
01:22:02,541 --> 01:22:11,585
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is that pretty much none of them, like all the people that practice, but without actually becoming arahants and so on, means something's wrong there.

961
01:22:12,385 --> 01:22:17,248
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's not that there was lack of teachings or lack of suttas or Buddhist instruction or that it's not clear enough.

962
01:22:18,109 --> 01:22:25,893
[SPEAKER_00]: It's that they never ever heard or learned on what level it needs to be applied and it was always stayed within the framework of management, unknowingly.

963
01:22:26,914 --> 01:22:27,695
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

964
01:22:27,955 --> 01:22:36,480
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to stay within that recognition of that liability, prey to suffering, which is pretty much the framework of anxiety.

965
01:22:38,753 --> 01:22:45,778
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why people would go mad if they stay on that level and often would, like, or quickly manage it because, yeah, they don't know any other way.

966
01:22:46,158 --> 01:22:56,206
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to... Sense-restraint virtue is what will prevent you from going mental.

967
01:22:56,286 --> 01:22:57,186
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, so there's two things.

968
01:22:57,306 --> 01:23:03,551
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, you said here... you said that sense-restraint and, like, morality is what prevents you from going mental.

969
01:23:03,571 --> 01:23:04,592
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

970
01:23:04,612 --> 01:23:07,434
[SPEAKER_02]: And you said that...

971
01:23:12,455 --> 01:23:14,817
[SPEAKER_00]: It will prevent you going mad on account of anxiety.

972
01:23:14,997 --> 01:23:16,558
[SPEAKER_00]: It will not uproot the anxiety.

973
01:23:18,680 --> 01:23:18,900
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

974
01:23:19,560 --> 01:23:20,881
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you can uproot it eventually.

975
01:23:24,864 --> 01:23:25,105
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

976
01:23:25,285 --> 01:23:32,070
[SPEAKER_02]: And to put in, like, basically, from, from, if I, if I understand well, like, if you, if you, to practice correctly,

977
01:23:32,560 --> 01:23:39,001
[SPEAKER_02]: If you want... if you practice rightly, and then you have to be always practicing with a view, basically, to the aim of... Uploading.

978
01:23:39,021 --> 01:23:41,542
[SPEAKER_02]: ...of getting out of suffering.

979
01:23:42,002 --> 01:23:42,222
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

980
01:23:42,422 --> 01:23:45,303
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, this has to be the aim constantly.

981
01:23:45,323 --> 01:23:46,003
[SPEAKER_00]: Uploading the liability, yeah.

982
01:23:46,023 --> 01:23:48,264
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, then everything should drive you towards this aim.

983
01:23:48,464 --> 01:23:49,104
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

984
01:23:50,784 --> 01:23:53,545
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, this... this is what I... this is what I understand.

985
01:23:53,645 --> 01:23:54,825
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, that you have to...

986
01:23:56,520 --> 01:24:01,206
[SPEAKER_02]: The aim should be, always, at every moment, not to get out of the hurt.

987
01:24:01,447 --> 01:24:01,807
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

988
01:24:01,927 --> 01:24:04,411
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not an abstract aim, in a sense.

989
01:24:04,551 --> 01:24:07,515
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like, oh, I must keep this thought in my mind.

990
01:24:07,875 --> 01:24:08,817
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the anxious aim.

991
01:24:09,357 --> 01:24:10,459
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the aim you feel.

992
01:24:10,519 --> 01:24:12,201
[SPEAKER_00]: Anxiety remains there.

993
01:24:12,978 --> 01:24:13,779
[SPEAKER_00]: Liable to it.

994
01:24:14,340 --> 01:24:15,861
[SPEAKER_00]: The aim is there, outlined.

995
01:24:16,262 --> 01:24:18,364
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to think it anymore in that sense.

996
01:24:18,685 --> 01:24:19,265
[SPEAKER_00]: What is my aim?

997
01:24:19,285 --> 01:24:19,846
[SPEAKER_00]: What is my aim?

998
01:24:20,187 --> 01:24:28,196
[SPEAKER_00]: But unless you have uncovered that liability of... that's anxious in its nature, then you have to drill and drill until you learn what dukkha is first.

999
01:24:35,463 --> 01:24:35,803
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

1000
01:24:35,823 --> 01:24:46,752
[SPEAKER_02]: So, may I first, like, well, about, like, making this aim clear and then taking your actions in order to bring, taking every action or taking every practice in order to bring you closer to this aim?

1001
01:24:48,294 --> 01:24:49,034
[SPEAKER_02]: This is correct.

1002
01:24:49,054 --> 01:24:50,415
[SPEAKER_02]: This is aim clear above.

1003
01:24:54,158 --> 01:25:01,004
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, feeling, like, do you mean that you should have to feel anxious constantly in practicing or no?

1004
01:25:01,893 --> 01:25:05,155
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you don't have to be, like, sort of trying to contrive anxiety.

1005
01:25:05,435 --> 01:25:07,836
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you're thinking correctly, you will feel uneasy.

1006
01:25:07,896 --> 01:25:15,400
[SPEAKER_00]: To what extent that uneasiness will be a threatening anxiety and fear or not, depends to what extent you basically developed your sīla and are able to hold it.

1007
01:25:15,980 --> 01:25:18,862
[SPEAKER_00]: But the recognition of the peril is...

1008
01:25:18,902 --> 01:25:27,446
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, part of understanding the danger is part of freeing yourself from sensuality, from the world, from the aggregates, from everything.

1009
01:25:27,486 --> 01:25:28,626
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the danger is still there.

1010
01:25:28,686 --> 01:25:29,447
[SPEAKER_00]: Arahant sees it.

1011
01:25:30,203 --> 01:25:37,146
[SPEAKER_00]: But yes, how dangerous that danger is for you, it's basically how much you have understood it or not.

1012
01:25:37,546 --> 01:25:38,707
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's still the same danger.

1013
01:25:39,527 --> 01:25:43,188
[SPEAKER_00]: So, initially, it's probably going to be very frightening and very anxious.

1014
01:25:44,309 --> 01:25:54,273
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you don't act out of it, don't break the sīla, sensory strength and that strength that you've accumulated through basically controlling your output, your behavior,

1015
01:25:59,386 --> 01:26:04,107
[SPEAKER_00]: Anxiety is a problem when it's threatening, when there is a risk of being overwhelmed by it.

1016
01:26:06,847 --> 01:26:12,228
[SPEAKER_00]: If you have sustained it long enough, there is less and less risk for that danger to overwhelm you.

1017
01:26:12,588 --> 01:26:13,928
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you get to see it even clearer.

1018
01:26:14,548 --> 01:26:17,269
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah, the peril, the danger becomes apparent.

1019
01:26:18,229 --> 01:26:22,670
[SPEAKER_00]: And when the danger is apparent, you have abandoned any passion towards that which is dangerous.

1020
01:26:24,844 --> 01:26:28,767
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that to me, it's completely not abstract or on the level of theories of figuring it out.

1021
01:26:28,867 --> 01:26:29,868
[SPEAKER_00]: It's actually felt.

1022
01:26:30,008 --> 01:26:41,497
[SPEAKER_00]: The danger is felt first, and then you, not being liable to that same danger, is also felt, which is when, ah, the danger becomes apparent, thus the freedom becomes apparent from it.

1023
01:26:45,480 --> 01:26:50,344
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how much you're gonna feel anxiety, and when, in what process, depends pretty much on

1024
01:26:51,591 --> 01:27:04,736
[SPEAKER_00]: How much samādhi you have developed, how well your virtue has been established, and how intense is your current recognition of samādhi... of, sorry, of anxiety, of liability to suffering.

1025
01:27:04,757 --> 01:27:06,057
[SPEAKER_00]: Sometimes will be more, sometimes...

1026
01:27:06,077 --> 01:27:07,458
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have good days, bad days.

1027
01:27:07,898 --> 01:27:10,879
[SPEAKER_00]: But that is... that is the field where you have to do the work.

1028
01:27:11,639 --> 01:27:12,260
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not optional.

1029
01:27:12,420 --> 01:27:13,000
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I'm saying.

1030
01:27:14,777 --> 01:27:16,399
[SPEAKER_00]: How much you're gonna suffer on account of this option?

1031
01:27:16,419 --> 01:27:17,039
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what Buddha said.

1032
01:27:17,059 --> 01:27:23,947
[SPEAKER_00]: Sometimes, for some monks, practice is pleasant, with pleasant results, painful, with pleasant results, and so on.

1033
01:27:25,008 --> 01:27:25,929
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's the same thing.

1034
01:27:26,930 --> 01:27:28,852
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like, oh, I have this approach or that approach.

1035
01:27:28,912 --> 01:27:33,357
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just, some minds are stronger, they will suffer less on account of the threat.

1036
01:27:34,318 --> 01:27:35,579
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they have more composure.

1037
01:27:36,701 --> 01:27:37,121
[SPEAKER_00]: Samādhi.

1038
01:27:37,841 --> 01:27:40,662
[SPEAKER_00]: Some minds are not that strong, so they'll suffer more.

1039
01:27:40,702 --> 01:27:45,344
[SPEAKER_00]: That's like Sariputta, who practiced through vipassana, not samādhi as much.

1040
01:27:46,104 --> 01:27:48,024
[SPEAKER_00]: His way was unpleasant and long.

1041
01:27:49,885 --> 01:27:53,486
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, yeah, he was the second to the Buddha, in terms of the foremost wisdom.

1042
01:27:55,827 --> 01:28:02,109
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you start actually practicing rightly, even the unpleasant way, you will recognize the liberating aspect of it.

1043
01:28:02,189 --> 01:28:03,329
[SPEAKER_00]: So you will have no problem.

1044
01:28:05,310 --> 01:28:09,254
[SPEAKER_00]: In basically enduring that anxiety, it doesn't matter how big it gets and how threatening it is.

1045
01:28:11,916 --> 01:28:14,458
[SPEAKER_00]: But in order to get there, you need to start rightly.

1046
01:28:14,939 --> 01:28:19,102
[SPEAKER_00]: And that will be hard for somebody who hasn't been practicing on that level.

1047
01:28:27,370 --> 01:28:30,532
[SPEAKER_02]: So, Bejas, if I... so I don't understand well what you're saying.

1048
01:28:30,552 --> 01:28:32,494
[SPEAKER_02]: You say that you have two, like,

1049
01:28:33,027 --> 01:28:50,993
[SPEAKER_02]: You have to feel anxious, feel anxiety, feel suffering, in the sense that you are... that you have no control over your life, and that you will get old, and that you will get sick, and that you will die no matter what you do, and all of this.

1050
01:28:52,594 --> 01:28:56,135
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you have to start from this point, in order to have...

1051
01:28:59,304 --> 01:29:06,992
[SPEAKER_00]: Keep the... have the aim and then keep the aim of... Keep the right context as a basis, yeah, for the correct direction of your practice, yeah.

1052
01:29:09,534 --> 01:29:10,375
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what I mean.

1053
01:29:10,395 --> 01:29:12,637
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why people don't keep that right context.

1054
01:29:12,697 --> 01:29:14,539
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because they choose something.

1055
01:29:14,599 --> 01:29:17,082
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because they don't want to feel that discomfort.

1056
01:29:23,739 --> 01:29:28,563
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not an abstract choice that they make, or some like a little mistake because they didn't have enough information.

1057
01:29:28,603 --> 01:29:36,450
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's existentially avoiding the recognition that you are within the perilous situation, without any means of escape.

1058
01:29:37,310 --> 01:29:38,271
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's very frightening.

1059
01:29:39,412 --> 01:29:42,074
[SPEAKER_00]: That's essentially fear of death, in a nutshell.

1060
01:29:42,755 --> 01:29:46,198
[SPEAKER_00]: All the people who are not necessarily even... might call it that way or be aware of it.

1061
01:30:01,564 --> 01:30:05,026
[SPEAKER_02]: It cut out there for a moment.

1062
