[SPEAKER_02]: Can you hear me now?

1063
01:30:08,707 --> 01:30:08,967
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

1064
01:30:09,728 --> 01:30:09,968
[SPEAKER_02]: Oops.

1065
01:30:11,048 --> 01:30:11,308
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay.

1066
01:30:11,748 --> 01:30:13,369
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay.

1067
01:30:14,990 --> 01:30:15,290
[SPEAKER_02]: So, yeah.

1068
01:30:15,310 --> 01:30:16,471
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it just cut there a little bit.

1069
01:30:16,491 --> 01:30:28,816
[SPEAKER_02]: The last part I heard was that you were saying how when people, like, start on the wrong footing, essentially, or they don't practice rightly, it's because of

1070
01:30:34,762 --> 01:30:38,704
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means you can always fix... you can always fix your footing at any given time.

1071
01:30:39,284 --> 01:30:40,224
[SPEAKER_00]: You can always fix that.

1072
01:30:40,264 --> 01:30:42,866
[SPEAKER_00]: You can always restart and re-establish the right framework.

1073
01:30:43,526 --> 01:30:54,050
[SPEAKER_00]: But the thing is, the more you give in to ignoring it because it's fundamentally frightening and unpleasant, the more you drift away and more incapable of accepting it, you are.

1074
01:30:54,170 --> 01:30:57,392
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the more effort in trying to outrun it, you invest it.

1075
01:30:58,107 --> 01:31:04,925
[SPEAKER_00]: So, theoretically, anybody can become an Arahant at any stage of life, but practically, if you spend 20-30 years more...

1076
01:31:05,808 --> 01:31:21,300
[SPEAKER_00]: Avoiding this, and even practicing while avoiding it, it would be virtually impossible for you, after all that invested effort, to basically just wipe your slate clean and re-accept that fundamental liability that will fill you with anxiety and make you, like, back onto the day one.

1077
01:31:22,100 --> 01:31:32,608
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, people won't do that in... for mundane things, let alone for something so profound and hard to uproot, such as, you know, existential anxiety, dukkha, fear.

1078
01:31:38,398 --> 01:31:47,650
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, I mean, that's why I think most people, they don't really practice for the aim of the goal, really.

1079
01:31:47,670 --> 01:31:55,520
[SPEAKER_02]: They don't practice for another reason or for, like, basically wanting to feel better or...

1080
01:31:57,098 --> 01:32:03,960
[SPEAKER_00]: And there is no... if they don't practice with that as a basis, the right basis, they don't practice dhamma, even if it's the dhamma they're doing.

1081
01:32:04,620 --> 01:32:05,680
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, the Buddha said it himself.

1082
01:32:06,140 --> 01:32:11,782
[SPEAKER_00]: Only, like, his teaching has only one taste, like the ocean has only one taste of salt.

1083
01:32:12,342 --> 01:32:13,502
[SPEAKER_00]: Taste, freedom from suffering.

1084
01:32:13,562 --> 01:32:14,082
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing else.

1085
01:32:15,123 --> 01:32:20,544
[SPEAKER_00]: So, suffering, freedom from it, is the only place where dhamma can be.

1086
01:32:20,564 --> 01:32:21,684
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I mean, it's not optional.

1087
01:32:21,704 --> 01:32:23,865
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not, oh, let me choose this or choose that.

1088
01:32:27,852 --> 01:32:28,592
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the only way.

1089
01:32:28,672 --> 01:32:34,795
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

1090
01:32:38,537 --> 01:32:40,278
[SPEAKER_02]: There was maybe just one more.

1091
01:32:40,358 --> 01:32:44,120
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm aware I've taken up a lot of your time already.

1092
01:32:45,501 --> 01:32:53,945
[SPEAKER_02]: And I would just like to maybe to ask on one more, one more point.

1093
01:33:01,086 --> 01:33:01,706
[SPEAKER_02]: A bit or two.

1094
01:33:04,567 --> 01:33:11,029
[SPEAKER_02]: So, like, if I can kind of summarize basically the, like, overall the different points that are mentioned.

1095
01:33:14,810 --> 01:33:19,711
[SPEAKER_02]: So, from the beginning, you have to start, like, with recognizing that you're sick.

1096
01:33:20,192 --> 01:33:26,518
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, the recognizing that you... that you're... what the nature of your problem is, and then you have to, like, make the aim clear.

1097
01:33:28,199 --> 01:33:34,725
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, if you're not practicing with the realization that you're sick and, like, we're needing to be healed, then you're not practicing right.

1098
01:33:34,905 --> 01:33:43,392
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to resist the temptation to manage the symptoms at the expense of the recognition of your illness that's still there, even if you remove the symptoms.

1099
01:33:43,913 --> 01:33:45,054
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but am I still alive?

1100
01:33:45,094 --> 01:33:45,374
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

1101
01:33:45,614 --> 01:33:46,455
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, I'm still ill.

1102
01:33:48,238 --> 01:33:51,040
[SPEAKER_00]: So, don't get complacent, even when things don't bother you.

1103
01:33:52,080 --> 01:33:56,522
[SPEAKER_00]: And certainly, don't make your practice revolve around dealing with things that bother you only.

1104
01:33:59,284 --> 01:33:59,524
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

1105
01:34:00,384 --> 01:34:00,565
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

1106
01:34:00,585 --> 01:34:02,566
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I mean, don't look just for a painkiller, basically.

1107
01:34:02,586 --> 01:34:04,187
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you want to go to the...

1108
01:34:05,450 --> 01:34:07,691
[SPEAKER_02]: You want to go to see... go to a doctor...

1109
01:34:07,971 --> 01:34:10,532
[SPEAKER_00]: If the pain is too much, take the painkiller.

1110
01:34:10,793 --> 01:34:16,235
[SPEAKER_00]: But don't forget that fundamentally the only reason you needed to take the painkiller and had pain is because you're still sick.

1111
01:34:16,455 --> 01:34:18,276
[SPEAKER_00]: There's still the underlying illness.

1112
01:34:18,296 --> 01:34:20,837
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

1113
01:34:20,937 --> 01:34:29,041
[SPEAKER_02]: And as long as you haven't cured the underlying illness, then you can continue to take painkillers, but the illness is still there and it's getting worse.

1114
01:34:29,461 --> 01:34:29,802
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

1115
01:34:29,962 --> 01:34:32,403
[SPEAKER_00]: And now you don't even have the symptoms to tell you how worse it is.

1116
01:34:33,918 --> 01:34:35,339
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah, exactly.

1117
01:34:35,419 --> 01:34:37,740
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's a big problem.

1118
01:34:39,301 --> 01:34:39,862
[SPEAKER_02]: I agree.

1119
01:34:42,203 --> 01:34:43,444
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you can feel so calm.

1120
01:34:43,844 --> 01:34:48,427
[SPEAKER_02]: You can make yourself feel very calm by just concentrating on the breath or something like that.

1121
01:34:48,447 --> 01:34:52,550
[SPEAKER_02]: And you can spend hours a day just concentrating on the sensation of breathing.

1122
01:34:52,910 --> 01:34:54,171
[SPEAKER_02]: And now you feel so much better.

1123
01:34:54,191 --> 01:34:55,812
[SPEAKER_02]: And you think everything's going so much better.

1124
01:34:55,852 --> 01:34:56,512
[SPEAKER_02]: You think you're fine.

1125
01:34:57,533 --> 01:35:01,095
[SPEAKER_02]: Now you stop searching for a way out because you think you're fine.

1126
01:35:01,720 --> 01:35:17,625
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why we said in other talks, most of today's meditation, Buddhist meditation practice, is just calming techniques, which is fine if you need to calm down, but don't confuse that for samādhi, that will result in pañña and complete wisdom and liberation.

1127
01:35:17,645 --> 01:35:19,646
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

1128
01:35:21,667 --> 01:35:30,470
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, the other point is then about, once you know what you're doing, then what does the practice consist of?

1129
01:35:30,490 --> 01:35:31,150
[SPEAKER_02]: At one point you said

1130
01:35:31,715 --> 01:35:33,937
[SPEAKER_02]: Morality is to stop yourself from going mad.

1131
01:35:37,520 --> 01:35:43,565
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't know if I have seen this...

1132
01:35:43,826 --> 01:35:45,687
[SPEAKER_02]: I think morality is essential.

1133
01:35:45,967 --> 01:35:48,670
[SPEAKER_02]: It's an essential base of practicing.

1134
01:35:48,710 --> 01:35:55,516
[SPEAKER_02]: Morality is in the sense of not having any wrongdoing in thoughts, words or actions.

1135
01:36:06,973 --> 01:36:27,592
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, what you were talking about, what you were saying, is that basically you need to kind of realize that the problem, like understand where the problem is, in the sense that you can become affected by anger, or that you can become greedy, or that you are

1136
01:36:30,655 --> 01:36:38,682
[SPEAKER_02]: or having desire for things in the first place, and not to just, like, want to kind of manage these things when they arise.

1137
01:36:38,762 --> 01:36:40,403
[SPEAKER_02]: So, this point is okay.

1138
01:36:47,329 --> 01:36:58,598
[SPEAKER_02]: And... yeah, so then the... and then the correct practice, like, to... what... yeah, what kind of still is, like, kind of trying to figure it out, is the...

1139
01:37:00,720 --> 01:37:12,826
[SPEAKER_02]: If you're practicing rightly, like, if you practice correctly, then you should see that you're practicing correctly, right?

1140
01:37:12,846 --> 01:37:19,970
[SPEAKER_02]: So, there's no need to have the same anxiety that was there in the beginning, if you know that you're practicing rightly.

1141
01:37:20,130 --> 01:37:20,410
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

1142
01:37:20,430 --> 01:37:23,652
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're not... See, you're not ignoring your liability to suffer.

1143
01:37:23,732 --> 01:37:25,913
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not ignoring the peril.

1144
01:37:26,611 --> 01:37:30,553
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not turning a blind eye, you're not distracting yourself from it, and it doesn't bother you as much.

1145
01:37:31,774 --> 01:37:36,116
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because you pretend it's not there, because it... you get used to it.

1146
01:37:37,997 --> 01:37:39,858
[SPEAKER_00]: Anxiety will stop bothering you when you get used to it.

1147
01:37:39,878 --> 01:37:44,520
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you are able to maintain composure in the face of it.

1148
01:37:45,140 --> 01:37:48,142
[SPEAKER_00]: And the only way to do that is to train composure in the face of anxiety.

1149
01:37:59,531 --> 01:38:20,102
[SPEAKER_02]: And to come back, just to just come back to the beginning about the... because you were telling the base of, like, the base of being able to practice mindfulness correctly is to have the the right kind of awareness, to be able to have awareness correctly, which is not about just, like,

1150
01:38:20,499 --> 01:38:32,887
[SPEAKER_02]: paying attention to sensation, or paying attention to sound, or paying attention to something else, nor is it about, like, analyzing your thoughts, or analyzing your, like, experience.

1151
01:38:34,968 --> 01:38:41,732
[SPEAKER_02]: And so now you were... so now you said the periphery, meaning the...

1152
01:38:43,972 --> 01:38:49,017
[SPEAKER_02]: Like what you said was the basis for that for on which you're paying attention in the first place.

1153
01:38:49,037 --> 01:38:50,078
[SPEAKER_00]: Periphery means the background.

1154
01:38:50,178 --> 01:38:52,580
[SPEAKER_00]: The background of whatever is currently attended in there.

1155
01:38:52,640 --> 01:38:55,383
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the mind is the background in itself.

1156
01:38:56,124 --> 01:39:01,068
[SPEAKER_00]: The intentionality, the possibilities, as we described, that's already the background in itself.

1157
01:39:01,249 --> 01:39:03,511
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what peripheral is.

1158
01:39:06,022 --> 01:39:06,983
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

1159
01:39:07,143 --> 01:39:25,093
[SPEAKER_00]: It can sound tactical and I think in those videos we use it for the sake of the context, but it's really the background of your currently engaged experience that you don't want to make into a foreground, because then, again, the point is not what is the foreground or what is the background.

1160
01:39:25,113 --> 01:39:32,658
[SPEAKER_00]: The point is to be aware of the background while there is whatever action, intention, presently enduring feeling and so on in the foreground.

1161
01:39:34,327 --> 01:39:37,329
[SPEAKER_00]: And that background is literally the knowledge of that situation, nothing else.

1162
01:39:38,069 --> 01:39:40,431
[SPEAKER_00]: No sensations, nothing specific.

1163
01:39:40,911 --> 01:39:43,092
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it's so hard to see, because it's non-specific.

1164
01:39:44,193 --> 01:39:45,874
[SPEAKER_00]: And what else is non-specific?

1165
01:39:45,994 --> 01:39:48,435
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the nature of things is non-specific.

1166
01:39:50,256 --> 01:40:01,983
[SPEAKER_00]: The nature of this specific cup, the nature of this specific object, is non-specific from the point of view of the specific object to which that object kind of pertains to.

1167
01:40:03,440 --> 01:40:05,842
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's, in essence, dhamma, the nature of things.

1168
01:40:06,462 --> 01:40:09,304
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's always found in that background where the knowledge is.

1169
01:40:17,329 --> 01:40:18,310
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

1170
01:40:18,330 --> 01:40:19,550
[SPEAKER_00]: Specifically, there is...

1171
01:40:19,570 --> 01:40:20,811
[SPEAKER_02]: Specifically, there is an insult.

1172
01:40:27,695 --> 01:40:30,897
[SPEAKER_00]: Angry voice, everything, all of that is quite specific.

1173
01:40:31,637 --> 01:40:35,539
[SPEAKER_00]: Non-specifically, there is a presence of suffering that I'm resisting.

1174
01:40:36,160 --> 01:40:38,341
[SPEAKER_00]: Non-specifically, there is discomfort.

1175
01:40:38,861 --> 01:40:44,004
[SPEAKER_00]: There are many different non-specific ways you can take, but it will be the same nature of this experience.

1176
01:40:44,124 --> 01:40:50,527
[SPEAKER_00]: So, fundamentally, the problem here is not what's being said, what's being heard, not even what's being felt.

1177
01:40:50,727 --> 01:40:51,748
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is that

1178
01:40:52,508 --> 01:40:58,312
[SPEAKER_00]: Within this, basically, experience as a whole, I'm resisting it and I have a view that I should not suffer.

1179
01:40:59,172 --> 01:41:01,354
[SPEAKER_00]: That I'm in control and I should not suffer.

1180
01:41:01,394 --> 01:41:02,255
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all implicit.

1181
01:41:04,036 --> 01:41:05,617
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the nature of that experience.

1182
01:41:06,097 --> 01:41:07,058
[SPEAKER_00]: That is the background.

1183
01:41:07,098 --> 01:41:11,861
[SPEAKER_00]: That is the the vessel in which you... and that's where you get... you get insulted.

1184
01:41:12,948 --> 01:41:18,676
[SPEAKER_00]: When you hear something immediately, because the framework ready for insult is already there.

1185
01:41:18,696 --> 01:41:26,907
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, framework ready to resist any form of discomfort that comes through the senses is already there, currently maintained by you.

